<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>What is EPDM roofing used for?</h3>
    <p>EPDM (ethylene propylene diene monomer) is a synthetic rubber membrane used on flat and low-slope roofs. It is the dominant material for commercial flat roofing and increasingly popular on residential flat-roof additions, garages, and extensions. EPDM is durable, flexible in cold weather, and typically lasts 25 to 30 years when properly installed.</p>

    <h3>Can I install EPDM myself?</h3>
    <p>Experienced DIYers can handle EPDM on smaller roofs. The material is lightweight and bondable with standard adhesives. The most critical steps are surface preparation and correctly bonding seams. Any gap in seam adhesion becomes a leak path within a year or two. For roofs over 500 sq ft or those with many penetrations, professional installation is worth the cost.</p>

    <h3>How long does EPDM roofing material last?</h3>
    <p>A properly installed EPDM roof lasts 25 to 35 years. Quality largely depends on the thickness of membrane you use. 45-mil is the minimum for residential use. 60-mil is standard for commercial applications and extends lifespan noticeably. Thicker membranes also provide better resistance to puncture from foot traffic.</p>
</section>

